+# Deploying to Base + IPFS
+
+Production = the TipJar contract on Base mainnet + a static site on IPFS that talks to it.
+Run everything from the repo root.
+
+> ⚠️ Step 1 spends real ETH on Base, and the deployed site moves real USDC. Double-check `TIP_JAR_OWNER`.
+
+## 0. Prerequisites
+
+```bash
+nvm use            # Node 22 (.nvmrc). Node 25 breaks `next build`.
+yarn install --immutable
+```
+
+- A deployer keystore with a few dollars of ETH on Base: `yarn generate` (new) or `yarn account:import` (existing), then check it with `yarn account`.
+- The address that should receive tips (ideally a Safe). It becomes the TipJar owner.
+- `packages/foundry/.env`: `ALCHEMY_API_KEY` (Base enabled) and `ETHERSCAN_API_KEY` (an Etherscan V2 key also covers Base).
+- `packages/nextjs/.env.local` (these values end up **public** in the bundle):
+
+  ```bash
+  NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY=<key restricted to Base mainnet>
+  NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=<your WalletConnect/Reown project id>
+  ```
+
+## 1. Deploy the contract to Base
+
+```bash
+TIP_JAR_OWNER=<tip recipient address> yarn deploy --network base
+(cd packages/foundry && make verify RPC_URL=base)
+```
+
+This writes the Base address (chain `8453`) into `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts`. Commit that file and `packages/foundry/broadcast/Deploy.s.sol/8453/`: the site build reads the address from there.
+
+Check the deployment onchain:
+
+```bash
+BASE_RPC=https://mainnet.base.org
+TIP_JAR=$(jq -r '.transactions[0].contractAddress' packages/foundry/broadcast/Deploy.s.sol/8453/run-latest.json)
+cast code $TIP_JAR -r $BASE_RPC | head -c 20; echo      # not "0x"
+cast call $TIP_JAR "usdc()(address)" -r $BASE_RPC        # 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+cast call $TIP_JAR "owner()(address)" -r $BASE_RPC       # your TIP_JAR_OWNER
+cast call $TIP_JAR "tipCount()(uint256)" -r $BASE_RPC    # 0
+```
+
+## 2. Build the static site
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+What this does (`packages/nextjs`):
+
+- Sets `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true`. `scaffold.config.ts` then targets **Base only**: no local chain, no burner wallet, no fork faucet.
+- Fails early if `deployedContracts.ts` has no TipJar on Base.
+- Lints, then runs `next build` with `output: "export"` + `trailingSlash: true` (every route becomes `<route>/index.html`) and unoptimized images.
+- Copies `404.html` to `ipfs-404.html`, the 404 page IPFS gateways look for.
+
+Output: `packages/nextjs/out/`.
+
+Check the build before uploading:
+
+```bash
+grep -rqi "$TIP_JAR" packages/nextjs/out/_next && echo "✅ Base TipJar address is in the bundle"
+python3 -m http.server 8080 -d packages/nextjs/out   # open http://localhost:8080 and check the feed loads from Base
+```
+
+## 3. Upload to IPFS
+
+With bgipfs (BuidlGuidl's IPFS cluster, already a dev dependency):
+
+```bash
+yarn workspace @se-2/nextjs bgipfs upload config init -u https://upload.bgipfs.com   # once; add -k <BGIPFS_API_KEY> if you have one
+yarn ipfs:upload                                                                     # prints "CID: <cid>"
+CID=<cid from the output>
+```
+
+Or with your own Kubo node plus any pinning service that supports the IPFS Pinning Service API (Pinata shown):
+
+```bash
+CID=$(ipfs add -r --cid-version 1 --quieter packages/nextjs/out)
+ipfs pin remote service add pinata https://api.pinata.cloud/psa <PINATA_JWT>   # once
+ipfs pin remote add --service=pinata --name=usdc-tip-jar --background=false $CID
+```
+
+### Which URL to share
+
+The export uses root-absolute asset paths (`/_next/...`), so the site has to be served from the **root of an origin**:
+
+- ✅ Subdomain gateway: `https://<CIDv1>.ipfs.dweb.link/`. `https://dweb.link/ipfs/<CID>/` redirects there, even for a `Qm…` CID.
+- ✅ Your own domain with DNSLink: TXT record `_dnslink.<domain>` = `dnslink=/ipfs/<CID>`, behind a DNSLink-aware gateway.
+- ❌ Path gateways that don't redirect (e.g. `https://ipfs.io/ipfs/<CID>/`): the HTML loads, but every script/style 404s.
+
+## 4. Post-deploy verification
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs:verify $CID              # default gateway: dweb.link. Or: yarn ipfs:verify $CID w3s.link
+```
+
+This checks that:
+
+- `/` and `/debug/` return `200 text/html`.
+- The site is served from a subdomain origin, so `/_next` assets resolve.
+- Every file in `packages/nextjs/out` is byte-identical (sha256) on the gateway. This proves the CID is exactly the build you checked, not a stale or partial upload.
+
+Then a manual smoke test on `https://dweb.link/ipfs/$CID/` (it redirects to the subdomain URL):
+
+1. **Connect Wallet** offers no burner wallet. After connecting, the button shows **Base** (or asks you to switch to it).
+2. **Latest tips** loads. The total matches `cast call $TIP_JAR "tipCount()(uint256)" -r $BASE_RPC`.
+3. Connect a real wallet on Base and send the smallest real tip (e.g. `0.01` USDC, approve + tip).
+4. The tip appears in the feed, and onchain:
+
+   ```bash
+   cast call $TIP_JAR "getLatestTips(uint256)((address,uint96,uint64,string)[])" 1 -r $BASE_RPC
+   cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "balanceOf(address)(uint256)" $(cast call $TIP_JAR "owner()(address)" -r $BASE_RPC) -r $BASE_RPC
+   ```
+
+5. Open a deep link directly: `https://<CIDv1>.ipfs.dweb.link/debug/` loads without going through `/`.
+
+## Updating the site
+
+Each build gives a new CID. Rebuild → upload → `yarn ipfs:verify <newCID>` → point DNSLink (if used) at the new CID. You only need to redeploy the contract if `TipJar.sol` changes. If you do, commit the new `deployedContracts.ts` before rebuilding.
